 because crontab is not available on this system:\\n  Error: /bin/sh: 1: crontab: not found\\n\\nOptions and next steps (pick one)\\n1) I can try to install cron/crontab on this machine and then add the crontab entry.\\n   - Caveat: Installing system packages typically requires sudo/root. If you want me to try, confirm and confirm whether sudo is permitted here.\\n   - Example commands (need sudo):\\n     sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y cron\\n     # then set crontab:\\n     (crontab -l 2>/dev/null | grep -v 'Docker weekly cleanup - managed by openhands' || true; \\\\\\n       echo '# Docker weekly cleanup - managed by openhands'; \\\\\\n       echo '0 3 * * 0 /workspace/scripts/docker-weekly-cleanup.sh >/dev/null 2>&1') | crontab -\\n\\n2) Use a systemd user timer (recommended if systemd user services are available)\\n   - I can create unit files (in your home or repo) and, if you want, attempt to enable/start them with:\\n     systemctl --user daemon-reload\\n     systemctl --user enable --now docker-weekly-cleanup.timer\\n   - This usually does not require sudo and is per-user. If you want me to create the service+timer files and try to enable them, tell me to proceed.\\n\\n3) Create a crontab snippet file and instructions for you to install manually\\n   - I can write /workspace/scripts/docker-cleanup.cron containing the managed cron lines and a short README with copy/paste instructions. You can install it later with:\\n     crontab /workspace/scripts/docker-cleanup.cron\\n\\n4) Don\u2019t schedule on this host; instead add automation in CI (e.g., GitHub Actions) \u2014 note that pruning on a CI runner will not clean your real host's docker, so this is usually not what you want.\\n\\nMy recommendation\\n- If you can run systemd user services here, option 2 (systemd user timer) is the cleanest and avoids needing sudo.\\n- Otherwise, if you (or I) can install cron with sudo, option 1 will work.\\n- If you prefer not to change the system now, I can create the crontab snippet + README so you can install it later (option 3).\\n\\nTell me which option you want me to take (and whether I may attempt to install packages with sudo). If you pick systemd timer or the crontab snippet, I will create the necessary files next.\", image_urls=None, wait_for_response=True, action='message', security_risk=None), MessageAction(content=\"It sounds like you have made quite a bit of progress. Given the guidelines we've set, I think option 2 seems the most promising and avoids the need for sudo permissions. Could you go ahead and create the systemd service and timer files? If anything goes wrong or if there's something you need along the way, just let me know. \\n\\nProceeding with systemd user timer feels like the safest and most sustainable choice.\", image_urls=None, wait_for_response=False, action='message', security_risk=None), RecallAction(recall_type='knowledge', query=\"It sounds like you have made quite a bit of progress. Given the guidelines we've set, I think option 2 seems the most promising and avoids the need for sudo permissions. Could you go ahead and create the systemd service and timer files? If anything goes wrong or if there's something you need along the way, just let me know. \\n\\nProceeding with systemd user timer feels like the safest and most sustainable choice.\", thought='', action='recall'), CmdRunAction(command='/bin/sh -lc \"set -euo pipefail\\nmkdir -p \\\\\"$HOME/.config/systemd/user\\\\\" /workspace/systemd/user\\ncat > \\\\\"$HOME/.config/systemd/user/docker-weekly-cleanup.service\\\\\" <<\\'SERVICE\\'\\n[Unit]\\nDescription=Docker weekly cleanup - runs /workspace/scripts/docker-weekly-cleanup.sh\\n\\n[Service]\\nType=oneshot\\nExecStart=/workspace/scripts/docker-weekly-cleanup.sh\\nSERVICE\\n\\ncat > \\\\\"$HOME/.config/systemd/user/docker-weekly-cleanup.timer\\\\\" <<\\'TIMER\\'\\n[Unit]\\nDescription=Timer for Docker weekly cleanup\\n\\n[Timer]\\nOnCalendar=Sun *-*-* 03:00:00\\nPersistent=true\\